[{"id":"VRCE-2-100-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":100,"verse":1,"english":"As Rāma cognizant of righteousness was thus encouraging Bharata, Jābāli, best of Brāhmaṇas, addressed him in words divorced from morality, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.852,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-108-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2100001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0f74f0f6b5ec0e308ddb47a7a445832237ea48e8bc2f3dba1aed0b48d8041105","compact":"e656c78126013d79a358b372686a7c020ab817944fbe45bf8f07d382c6f78b90","nasal":"07787b375088bbd5e8d4efe6cce4e2f5f597ccd9bdbc72cc2c98f40ee46fcb38"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-100-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":100,"verse":2,"english":"O Rāghava, endowed with a noble understanding and leading a life of asceticism, do not suffer your intellect to entertain inanities, like any low person.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.766,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-108-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2100002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"95de541e00133afb9fb4203800e8b1a0815c88195d5df5733c6a9ae7a41efbb2","compact":"dd143268e17fea5fd35a9415f2c83ac813179bc797b469747e624db9f355ef38","nasal":"dd143268e17fea5fd35a9415f2c83ac813179bc797b469747e624db9f355ef38"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-100-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":100,"verse":3,"english":"Who is whose friend ? And to what is one entitled and by virtue of what relation? And who is such ? Since a creature is born alone and dies alone, a person that cherishes his father and mother with affection, must, O Rāma, be looked upon as a madman.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.793,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-108-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2100003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M.